  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "Rotavirus is a highly contagious virus causing severe, watery diarrhea, vomiting, fever, and abdominal pain, primarily in infants and young children. Transmitted via the fecal-oral route or contaminated surfaces, it frequently leads to severe dehydration and hospitalization. Treatment focuses on hydration, as no specific medicine exists, but vaccines are effective for prevention.Key Details About RotavirusSymptoms: Intense, watery diarrhea (often 6+ times in 24 hours), vomiting, high fever (>102∘𝐹), stomach pain, and dehydration. Symptoms usually appear 1-2 days after exposure and last 3-8 days.Transmission: Highly contagious, spreading via the fecal-oral route—often when contaminated hands, objects (toys), or food touch the mouth. It can survive on surfaces for weeks.Complications: Severe dehydration, which can be fatal, is the main complication. It causes 20-40 deaths and 50,000+ hospitalizations in the US annually.Diagnosis: Doctors typically diagnose it based on symptoms, but it can be confirmed via a stool test.Treatment: There is no specific medication to treat the virus. Management focuses on preventing dehydration by increasing fluid intake or using IV fluids.Prevention: Vaccination is the most effective method, with two safe and effective vaccines (RotaTeq and Rotarix) approved for infants. Frequent handwashing is crucial.Rotavirus is most common from January to June. While it affects all ages, it is most severe in children, particularly those unvaccinated.",
